1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
This one's genuinely advanced and code-literate: the actual llama-server flags the app can pass, what each does, and why they're safe to leave alone if you never touch this screen.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,369
Every flag on this screen is opt-in.

3
00:00:08,369 --> 00:00:12,551
Leave a field unset and nothing is emitted for it on the command line — the server just uses its own defaults.

4
00:00:12,551 --> 00:00:17,000
That single rule is what lets an older llama-server build launch cleanly even with a newer app version pointed at it.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,592
Threads sets CPU thread count — best set near your physical core count, not your logical thread count.

6
00:00:21,592 --> 00:00:24,833
Flash attention is close to a free win almost everywhere, so turn it on.

7
00:00:24,833 --> 00:00:31,000
Mlock pins the model in RAM so it can't be swapped to disk; no-mmap loads it fully into RAM up front instead of memory-mapping it lazily.

8
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,256
The one worth knowing by heart: MoE offload.

9
00:00:33,256 --> 00:00:39,256
Cpu-moe pushes every expert layer of a mixture-of-experts model onto system RAM; n-cpu-moe N pushes just the first N.

10
00:00:39,256 --> 00:00:47,000
Attention stays on the GPU, the experts sit in RAM — that's the trick for running a big MoE model on a small GPU that could never hold the whole thing.

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:47,303
Llama.

12
00:00:47,303 --> 00:00:49,775
cpp auto-fits itself to your hardware by default.

13
00:00:49,775 --> 00:00:58,000
Fit off turns that auto-fit off entirely if you want manual control, and fit-target sets the memory margin in megabytes it leaves free per GPU when auto-fit is on.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,521
This isn't just a claim — the launch_tests module in local.

15
00:01:00,521 --> 00:01:06,248
rs unit-tests exactly this: an empty options struct produces an empty argument list, and each flag appears only when its field is set.

16
00:01:06,248 --> 00:01:08,000
That's the opt-in design, proven in code.

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,861
The gotcha: these flags only take effect on an engine new enough to know them.

18
00:01:11,861 --> 00:01:18,000
If you set a flag and see no difference, the first thing to check isn't your settings — it's the llama-server build version.

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:19,125
Local models covered.

20
00:01:19,125 --> 00:01:24,000
Next we leave the machine entirely and connect two of your own devices together — the mesh.

